WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.496
A frozen test set that matches production.

2
00:00:02.776 --> 00:00:10.925
Pull the threads together into one rule: the test set is a measuring instrument, so build it to match what you’ll measure, then stop touching it.

3
00:00:11.205 --> 00:00:12.315
Match production.

4
00:00:12.595 --> 00:00:18.376
Sample test documents from the same sources, time period, and mix your deployed model will see.

5
00:00:18.656 --> 00:00:32.373
If production is 40 percent chat messages, the test set should be roughly 40 percent chat messages, plus deliberate extra coverage of the rare cases you care about, reported separately so they don’t distort the headline number.

6
00:00:32.653 --> 00:00:34.211
Label it with the most care.

7
00:00:34.491 --> 00:00:38.480
Every test label gets human review, two reviewers if you can afford it.

8
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.451
Model-drafted labels never enter the test set unreviewed.

9
00:00:42.731 --> 00:00:44.117
Freeze and version it.

10
00:00:44.397 --> 00:00:47.213
Record a fingerprint of the test file in the manifest.

11
00:00:47.493 --> 00:00:52.016
Any change produces a new version with a new fingerprint and a note saying why.

12
00:00:52.296 --> 00:00:53.661
Refresh deliberately.

13
00:00:53.941 --> 00:00:56.907
Production drifts and repeated looks bias the score.

14
00:00:57.187 --> 00:01:06.573
Periodically build a new test version from fresh production samples, and score current models on both versions so you can tell model changes from test changes.

15
00:01:06.853 --> 00:01:11.291
The evals FAQ calls stale gold data out as a common failure.

16
00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.021
Report with error bars.

17
00:01:13.301 --> 00:01:25.227
State the test-set size and the margin of error (Studio 3, week 3; Miller’s paper on error bars for evals), so nobody reads a two-point difference on 200 examples as a result.
